                        "form": "dodmen"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "c3803d64-5e31-3d77-ba7c-35d609f2f3fd",
                        "entry_id": "565da782-1e8b-3364-8c94-6c88b7c61cef",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A land-based snail."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "East-Anglia",
                            "dialectal"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "58bad139-de51-4842-9f59-2b8d62718f53",
                                "sense_id": "c3803d64-5e31-3d77-ba7c-35d609f2f3fd",
                                "text": "Yt is as great pyte to se a woman wepe / As yt is to se a sely dodman crepe, / Or, as ye wold say, a sely goose go barefote."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "bb413f63-2fc1-41e6-b365-4ab745b0e11e",
                                "sense_id": "c3803d64-5e31-3d77-ba7c-35d609f2f3fd",
                                "text": "In that a Snayl or Dodman, vvhich is not only not vvarm, but to our feeling, very cold, is fain to brood its as cold svveatty eggs, neſted upon a cold vvet earth, beſpievving them about vvith the fuzze of a cold clammy froth, in coldish [d]raughty vveather, and all making vvay to a kind and timely hatching of them: […] I dare undertake to light ſooner of that vvarmth and reek and air, that vvill hatch an hens egge, than that cold and devv and clammineſs, that goes to the hatching of a ſnails."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "92150b12-7cda-4caf-93fd-b05f50744467",
                                "sense_id": "c3803d64-5e31-3d77-ba7c-35d609f2f3fd",
                                "text": "A snail is also called Dodman in Suffolk and Norfolk."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "fd1724e5-e00a-4aea-a8db-b0c009fb0dd3",
                                "sense_id": "c3803d64-5e31-3d77-ba7c-35d609f2f3fd",
                                "text": "\"[…] I'm a reg'lar Dodman, I am,\" said Mr. Peggotty, by which he meant snail, and this was his allusion to being slow to go, for he had attempted to go after every sentence, and had somehow or other come back again; \"but I wish you both well, and I wish you happy!\""
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "49f8b18e-3914-3adb-8d59-620c4d3e7738",
                        "entry_id": "565da782-1e8b-3364-8c94-6c88b7c61cef",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A snail's shell."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "East-Anglia",
                            "dialectal"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "9a141156-9cd1-4dba-9d13-25ff4ca3d2f6",
                                "sense_id": "49f8b18e-3914-3adb-8d59-620c4d3e7738",
                                "text": "DODMAN. A snail. Norfolk. Also, a snail-shell."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "e2731303-5bc8-3c0b-a08c-29e2474cb2de",
                        "entry_id": "565da782-1e8b-3364-8c94-6c88b7c61cef",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Any shellfish which casts its shell, such as a lobster."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "East-Anglia",
                            "dialectal"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "540a26c6-e271-42d1-872d-e214869ccdb1",
                                "sense_id": "e2731303-5bc8-3c0b-a08c-29e2474cb2de",
                                "text": "The Creatures that caſt their Skin are, the Snake, the Viper, the Grashopper, the Lizard, the Silk-worm, &c. Thoſe that caſt their Shell are, the Lobſter, the Crab, the Cra-fish, the Hodmandod or Dodman, the Tortoise, &c. The old Skins are found, but the old Shells never: So as it is like they ſcale off, and crumble away by degrees."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4f9fca0a-daa4-4b7c-a4bc-0b6845d1cd47",
                                "sense_id": "e2731303-5bc8-3c0b-a08c-29e2474cb2de",
                                "text": "HOD′MANDOD, n., A shell-fish, otherwise called dodman."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "75ad775a-fb8c-3bfb-a4e6-b18e603dd0a2",
                        "entry_id": "565da782-1e8b-3364-8c94-6c88b7c61cef",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A surveyor."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "East-Anglia",
                            "dialectal",
                            "rare"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "505a0999-39ca-4158-943b-8aa003d14082",
                                "sense_id": "75ad775a-fb8c-3bfb-a4e6-b18e603dd0a2",
                                "text": "At Wilmington in Sussex, the Long Man, with his 240 feet length cut into the turf on the hill-side[…], the largest and perhaps the earliest representation of prehistoric man in England, carries two staves. Now the soldier carries but one spear, the shepherd one crook, the pedestrian one staff, the farmer one pike. The surveyor alone carries two rods. The Long Man is the dod-man, the prehistoric surveyor."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
